S158 LRC14 DANGER-COUNT MOMENT-DUAL PROBE
==============================================================================
max_degree=13, one_swap_limit=80
[0] Assumption challenge / quotient declaration
  considered vertices:
    runners, danger arcs, exact endpoints, safe intervals, lift packets,
    danger-count states N=0..13, factorial moments, polynomial duals,
    Fourier modes, boundary owners, proof obligations.
  chosen primary vertices:
    danger-count states and moment-dual certificates, not runners or lifts.
  quotient preserves:
    the cover predicate N(t)>=1, exact Haar distribution of N, AP/GW
    equality visibility, and low-degree dual proof obligations.
  quotient destroys:
    where the safe interval sits, which endpoint owns a wall, and Q/R
    lift geometry; these must be recovered only after the moment gate.
  challenged assumption:
    the next proof has to be an interval/lift construction.  A cover has
    a purely count-theoretic shadow: N(t)>=1 everywhere.

[1] Exact danger-count dual theorem
  Let N(t)=#{s in S: ||s t||<1/14}.  Then
    safe_mu(S) = mu(N=0).
  For any polynomial P on {0,...,13} with
    P(0)=1 and P(n)<=0 for every n>=1,
  the pointwise inequality 1_{N=0} >= P(N) gives
    safe_mu(S) >= E[P(N)].
  Using the factorial basis P(n)=1+sum y_k*C(n,k),
  E[P(N)] depends only on the moments E[C(N,k)].

